---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { routePath } from '../../i18n/routes';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const p = (key: Parameters<typeof routePath>[1]) => routePath(locale, key);

const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What local SEO actually is' },
  { id: 'difference', label: 'How it differs from classic SEO' },
  { id: 'nap', label: 'NAP consistency and directories' },
  { id: 'site', label: 'The role of the website' },
  { id: 'multi', label: 'Multi-city and multi-location setups' },
  { id: 'programme', label: 'The 90-day programme' },
  { id: 'measure', label: 'What we measure' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between local SEO and normal SEO?',
    a: 'Normal SEO targets one result list. In local search there are two races: the map pack (three businesses) and the organic results underneath. They rank on different signals — in the map pack the business profile and proximity dominate.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long until I see results?',
    a: 'Most businesses see first movement in four to eight weeks, with results compounding over three to six months. Fixing a root problem on the profile can produce a faster jump; dense city centres take longer.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do you guarantee rankings?',
    a: 'No one can guarantee a ranking; Google itself says so plainly. What we guarantee is the work and the measurement: every week you see what we did and how your rankings moved. There is no contract, so if you are not happy you simply don’t continue next month.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Could I do this myself?',
    a: 'The basic profile fixes, yes. What gets hard is consistency and measurement: keeping the review flow alive, seeing what is actually happening across the grid, and reacting to competitor moves. Most businesses stall on rhythm, not strategy.',
  },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  locale={locale}
  route="services/local-seo"
  title="Local SEO Service | Wiserrise"
  description="A local SEO programme that combines profile, website and external trust signals to reach the top three on Google Maps and local search."
  eyebrow="Service"
  h1="Local SEO"
  lede="Local SEO is not the name of one task; it is a programme that aims your profile, your site and your external trust signals at the same target. This page explains how we build it and what we measure."
  serviceName="Local SEO"
  breadcrumb={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'Services', href: p('services') },
  ]}
  toc={toc}
  faq={faq}
  related={[
    { label: 'Google Business Profile Management', href: p('services/gbp') },
    { label: 'Map Rank Tracking', href: p('services/rank-tracking') },
    { label: 'Technical SEO', href: p('services/technical-seo') },
  ]}
  ctaLabel="Get My Free Audit →"
  ctaHref="/#contact"
>
  <h2 id="what">What local SEO actually is</h2>

  <p>
    When someone searches "dentist near me", Google produces two separate lists: a map with
    three businesses on top, and classic organic results below. Those lists rank by different
    rules, and most of the clicks go to the one on top.
  </p>

  <p>
    Local SEO is the whole body of work required to be visible in both. In the map pack the
    <a href={p('services/gbp')}>business profile</a> is decisive; in the organic list it is
    your site's content and technical health. Running them separately is a common mistake,
    because Google evaluates the same business entity in both and the signals feed each other.
  </p>

  <h2 id="difference">How it differs from classic SEO</h2>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th></th>
          <th>Classic SEO</th>
          <th>Local SEO</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Where you compete</strong></td>
          <td>One organic list</td>
          <td>Map pack + organic list</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Effect of location</strong></td>
          <td>Low</td>
          <td>Decisive — the searcher's position changes the ranking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Main asset</strong></td>
          <td>The website</td>
          <td>The business profile plus the website</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Unit of measurement</strong></td>
          <td>One ranking number</td>
          <td>A distribution of rankings across a grid</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Conversion shape</strong></td>
          <td>Site visit</td>
          <td>Call, directions, message — often without visiting the site</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    That last row is what most businesses miss. A large share of local demand arrives as a
    direct call or a directions request without the site ever being opened. A business
    looking only at site analytics therefore systematically underestimates what local SEO
    returns.
  </p>

  <h2 id="nap">NAP consistency and directories</h2>

  <p>
    NAP stands for name, address and phone. Every time Google sees the same business across
    different sources, it accumulates confidence that the business is real. Inconsistency
    between those sources — a different spelling here, an old phone number there — weakens
    that confidence.
  </p>

  <p>
    The work is to find every source the business appears in and lock it to a single form:
    industry directories, map services, social profiles and any leftover records from an old
    website. For address-hidden businesses the address field stays empty, which makes it even
    more critical that the name and phone are byte-identical everywhere.
  </p>

  <h2 id="site">The role of the website</h2>

  <p>
    However strong the profile is, the top three gets hard in competitive cities when the
    site does not support it. Google folds the linked website into its relevance calculation:
    which services the site talks about, which place names appear, and whether the pages
    genuinely explain those services.
  </p>

  <p>
    So the site side of the programme runs three tracks:
    <a href={p('services/technical-seo')}>technical SEO</a> makes sure the site can be crawled
    and indexed, <a href={p('services/on-page-seo')}>on-page SEO</a> pins each page to a single
    search intent, and <a href={p('services/content')}>content production</a> turns uncovered
    services and queries into pages.
  </p>

  <h2 id="multi">Multi-city and multi-location setups</h2>

  <p>
    Local SEO for a single-location business is not the same job as for one with three
    branches. Branch setups need a separate profile per location, a separate review pool and
    a separate location page on the site. Those pages being copies of each other is the
    most common — and most expensive — mistake we see.
  </p>

  <p>
    For businesses with no branches that nevertheless serve several cities, the situation is
    different: opening additional profiles is a policy violation. The right route is to build
    city-level visibility on the site — provided each city page carries real information about
    that city. Template pages with the city name swapped are treated as doorway pages and
    damage the site as a whole.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>The bar for creating a city page:</strong> if you have nothing real to say about
      that city (local competitor analysis, a case from there, region-specific data), do not
      create the page. Ten good city pages beat fifty templates.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="programme">The 90-day programme</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Weeks 0–2 — Measure and diagnose.</strong> Baseline grid rankings, profile
      audit, competitor category and review gap, technical state of the site, NAP scan.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Weeks 2–4 — Root fixes.</strong> Category and service structure, indexing
      blockers, missing structured data, directory inconsistencies. Most of the jump usually
      comes from this stage.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Weeks 4–8 — Content and signal building.</strong> Missing service pages, the
      profile post calendar, switching on the review flow.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Weeks 8–12 — Consolidation.</strong> Targeted fixes for the areas still weak on
      the grid, tightening internal links, reacting to competitor movement.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="measure">What we measure</h2>

  <p>
    We do not send a single ranking screenshot, because local rankings change with the
    searcher's location and a measurement from one point does not represent reality. Instead
    we report three sources together:
  </p>

  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>
      <a href={p('services/rank-tracking')}>Grid rankings</a> — where you place across 49
      points laid over the city, tracked over time
    </li>
    <li>Profile interactions — searches, direction requests, calls and messages</li>
    <li>Search Console — which queries you appear for, clicks and average position</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Read together, these catch situations like "rankings improved but the phone isn't ringing"
    or "traffic grew but from the wrong city" early, so the programme can be corrected.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="cta">
    <h2>Where do you actually <span class="highlight">rank</span> in your city?</h2>
    <p>The free audit maps your grid rankings, profile gaps and the distance to your competitors.</p>
  </Fragment>
</ContentLayout>
